\begin{abstract}
We prove that the consistently oriented double ray is ubiquitous if and only if it is ubiquitous restricted to the class of one-ended digraphs.
Additionally, we prove the same equivalence for the disjoint union of a consistently forward and a consistently backward oriented ray. 
Furthermore, we discuss the connection between these two ubiquity problems.
\end{abstract}

For digraphs, much less is known, even for the subdigraph relation, and for the rest of this article, we shall only consider the property of being ubiquitous under that relation.
While Zuther~\cite{Z1997}*{Theorem 2.17} proved that the consistently oriented ray is ubiquitous, Gut et al.~\cite{GKR2024}*{Theorem 1.1} proved the same for all possible orientations of the undirected ray.
