stem_triviality.open_answer:
Let \(N\) and \(N^*\) denote the a.s. numbers of infinite clusters in \(\mu_{\hp}\) and \(\mu^*_{\hp}\), respectively. Then the complete classification is:

\[
(N,N^*)\in \{(1,0),(0,1),(1,\infty),(\infty,1),(\infty,\infty)\}.
\]

Equivalently, almost surely exactly one of the following occurs:

- \(\mu_{\hp}\) has a unique infinite cluster and \(\mu^*_{\hp}\) has none;
- \(\mu^*_{\hp}\) has a unique infinite cluster and \(\mu_{\hp}\) has none;
- \(\mu_{\hp}\) has a unique infinite cluster and \(\mu^*_{\hp}\) has infinitely many;
- \(\mu^*_{\hp}\) has a unique infinite cluster and \(\mu_{\hp}\) has infinitely many;
- both \(\mu_{\hp}\) and \(\mu^*_{\hp}\) have infinitely many infinite clusters.

In particular, the pairs \((0,0)\), \((1,1)\), \((0,\infty)\), and \((\infty,0)\) cannot occur.

